      "math_academy_id": "q-77952",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Given that $\\{i,j,k\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-4,-1⟩,$ $⟨-3,-2,5⟩,$ and $⟨1,0,0⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
